    "answer": {
      "short": "$181^2$.\n\nExpand the hinted expression and recognise $(x^2+3x+1)^2$, then substitute $x=12$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x(x+1)(x+2)(x+3)+1&=(x^2+3x)(x^2+3x+2)+1\\\\x(x+1)(x+2)(x+3)+1&=x^4+6x^3+11x^2+6x+1\\\\x(x+1)(x+2)(x+3)+1&=(x^2+3x+1)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute $x=12$:\n$$\\begin{align*}12\\times13\\times14\\times15+1&=(12^2+3\\times12+1)^2\\\\12\\times13\\times14\\times15+1&=(144+36+1)^2\\\\12\\times13\\times14\\times15+1&=181^2.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $13^2<181<14^2$, only the primes $2,3,5,7,11,13$ need checking. The number $181$ is not divisible by $2,3$ or $5$, and $181=7\\times25+6=11\\times16+5=13\\times13+12$. Thus $181$ is prime, so the prime factorisation is $181^2$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
